WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.040
Every clue so far favoured separate cells.

2
00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.020
None reached the membrane.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.780
What would it take?

4
00:00:10.383 --> 00:00:12.883
Suppose you could see the membrane at a contact.

5
00:00:13.803 --> 00:00:15.243
What would you see if Golgi were right?

6
00:00:16.103 --> 00:00:17.203
And if Cajal were right?

7
00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:28.743
If the branches are fused, the outer membrane runs on around both, and no membrane

8
00:00:28.743 --> 00:00:29.743
crosses the junction.

9
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:34.603
If the cells are separate, two membranes face each other across a gap.

10
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:37.683
Each cytoplasm stops at its own membrane.

11
00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:46.277
The electron microscope could show it, in thin sections of nervous tissue.

12
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.780
In 1955, De Robertis and Bennett examined synapses in frog and earthworm.

13
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.540
In 1956, Sanford Palay examined the rat brain.

14
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:02.190
At the chemical synapses they examined, they saw the second sketch.

15
00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.250
Two membranes stayed separate across a cleft 10 to 20 nanometres wide.

16
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.270
Small vesicles crowded the terminal.

17
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.430
In such images, the receiving membrane also looks thickened.

18
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.210
Under light, that whole cleft sits inside one blur.

19
00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.010
The electron microscope replaced the blur with two membranes.

20
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:26.953
The question was settled, about eighty years after the black reaction.

21
00:01:27.983 --> 00:01:29.883
Some neurons are joined more closely.

22
00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:34.843
At an electrical synapse, channels called gap junctions cross both membranes.

23
00:01:35.917 --> 00:01:39.817
Ions and small molecules pass straight from one cell into the next.

24
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.740
Does this bring back Golgi's network?

25
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.840
What would you inject into one cell to find out?

26
00:01:52.183 --> 00:01:56.323
A small tracer such as Neurobiotin passes through to coupled neighbours.

27
00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:03.147
A 10-kilodalton dextran, nearly 5 nanometres across, stays in the injected cell.

28
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:08.310
In one shared cytoplasm, the dextran would spread too, given time.

29
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.950
The electron microscope then confirms two membranes.

30
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.350
In vertebrates, Golgi's network does not exist.

31
00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.010
Coupling through pores that can close does.

32
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.130
There are exceptions outside vertebrates.

33
00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.510
The squid giant axon forms from fused fibres.

34
00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.790
A comb jelly's nerve net shares one membrane.

35
00:02:32.833 --> 00:02:35.513
A touch is not a fusion, and neither is a pore.

36
00:02:36.473 --> 00:02:40.793
Separate cells, joined at specialized contacts, are what development has to wire.
